[
  {
    "id": 1,
    "week": 1,
    "title": "Sounds & Spelling",
    "subtitle": "Read Kinyarwanda the way it is written",
    "theme": "Pronunciation",
    "blocks": [
      { "type": "heading", "heading": "The Five Vowels" },
      { "type": "paragraph", "text": "Kinyarwanda is written with the Latin alphabet and has five vowels. Each letter keeps one sound wherever it appears, so once you know them you can read any word aloud." },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "tableHeaders": ["Letter", "Sounds like", "Example"],
        "tableRows": [
          ["a", "father", "amata (milk)"],
          ["e", "bed", "ejo (tomorrow / yesterday)"],
          ["i", "see", "inka (cow)"],
          ["o", "or", "oya (no)"],
          ["u", "food", "umuntu (person)"]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "callout",
        "calloutTitle": "What the spelling does not show",
        "lines": [
          "Every vowel can be short or long, and every syllable is said on a high or a low tone. Neither is written, so two words can look identical on the page and still sound different.",
          "You do not need to master this now. Listen for it whenever you hear Kinyarwanda spoken, and copy what you hear."
        ]
      },
      { "type": "heading", "heading": "Consonants Worth Knowing" },
      { "type": "paragraph", "text": "Most consonants sound as they do in English. These are the ones that will surprise you." },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "tableHeaders": ["Letters", "Sounds like", "Example"],
        "tableRows": [
          ["c", "ch in church", "icumi (ten)"],
          ["j", "s in measure", "ijoro (night)"],
          ["ny", "ny in canyon", "inyama (meat)"],
          ["sh", "sh in shoe", "ishuri (school)"],
          ["r", "a quick tap, between r and l", "amahoro (peace)"],
          ["rw", "r, then a light g, then w", "u Rwanda (Rwanda)"],
          ["bw", "b, then a light g, then w", "bwiza (nice)"]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "callout",
        "calloutTitle": "One sound for r and l",
        "lines": [
          "Kinyarwanda does not tell r and l apart. Nearly every word is spelled with r, and the sound is a single light tap of the tongue."
        ]
      },
      { "type": "heading", "heading": "Words Start With a Vowel" },
      { "type": "paragraph", "text": "Look at the nouns in this lesson: amazi, inka, umuntu, igitabo. Almost all of them open with a, i or u. That opening vowel is part of the noun, and you will see later how it changes with the kind of thing being named." },
      { "type": "paragraph", "text": "Read aloud: amazi, amata, umuntu, inka, inzu, ishuri, inshuti, igitabo, ijoro, inyama." },
      {
        "type": "quickCheck",
        "calloutTitle": "Quick check",
        "questions": [
          { "prompt": "The 'c' in 'icumi' sounds like:", "options": ["ch in church", "k in kite", "s in sun", "c in cat"], "answer": "ch in church", "explain": "In Kinyarwanda, c is always the 'ch' sound." },
          { "prompt": "The 'a' in 'amata' sounds like the 'a' in:", "options": ["father", "cat", "cake", "about"], "answer": "father", "explain": "Each vowel letter keeps one sound." },
          { "prompt": "Which of these is NOT shown in Kinyarwanda spelling?", "options": ["Tone", "The vowel a", "The sound sh", "The sound ny"], "answer": "Tone", "explain": "Tone and vowel length are heard but not written." }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "vocabulary": [
      { "swahili": "amazi", "english": "water" },
      { "swahili": "amata", "english": "milk" },
      { "swahili": "umuntu", "english": "person" },
      { "swahili": "inka", "english": "cow" },
      { "swahili": "inzu", "english": "house" },
      { "swahili": "ishuri", "english": "school" },
      { "swahili": "inshuti", "english": "friend" },
      { "swahili": "igitabo", "english": "book" },
      { "swahili": "ijoro", "english": "night" },
      { "swahili": "inyama", "english": "meat" }
    ],
    "quiz": [
      { "id": 1, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "How many vowel letters does Kinyarwanda have?", "options": ["Three", "Five", "Seven", "Ten"], "answer": "Five", "hint": null },
      { "id": 2, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "What does 'amazi' mean?", "options": ["Milk", "Water", "Meat", "Night"], "answer": "Water", "hint": null },
      { "id": 3, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "Which word means 'friend'?", "options": ["inshuti", "ishuri", "inzu", "inka"], "answer": "inshuti", "hint": null },
      { "id": 4, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "The 'j' in 'ijoro' sounds like:", "options": ["j in jam", "s in measure", "y in yes", "h in hat"], "answer": "s in measure", "hint": null },
      { "id": 5, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "What does 'igitabo' mean?", "options": ["School", "House", "Book", "Person"], "answer": "Book", "hint": null }
    ],
    "xpReward": 100
  },
  {
    "id": 2,
    "week": 1,
    "title": "Greetings",
    "subtitle": "Say hello at any time of day",
    "theme": "Greetings",
    "blocks": [
      { "type": "heading", "heading": "Hello, Morning to Evening" },
      { "type": "paragraph", "text": "Greeting people matters in Rwanda: you greet first and get to your business second. The greeting you choose depends on the time of day and on how long it has been since you last met." },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "tableHeaders": ["Kinyarwanda", "English"],
        "tableRows": [
          ["Mwaramutse", "Good morning"],
          ["Mwiriwe", "Good afternoon / Good evening"],
          ["Muraho", "Hello (after some time apart)"],
          ["Bite?", "Hi / What's up? (informal)"],
          ["Amakuru?", "How are you?"],
          ["Ni meza", "Fine"],
          ["Murakoze", "Thank you"]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "callout",
        "calloutTitle": "Amakuru? — Ni meza",
        "lines": [
          "Amakuru means 'news', so the question is really 'What's the news?'. The standard answer, Ni meza, means 'It is good'.",
          "Add cyane for 'very': Ni meza cyane. Murakoze cyane is 'thank you very much'."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "callout",
        "calloutTitle": "Muraho is not for every hello",
        "lines": [
          "Muraho is for someone you have not seen for a while. For a person you saw earlier today, use Mwaramutse or Mwiriwe, or Bite with a friend."
        ]
      },
      { "type": "heading", "heading": "One Person or Several" },
      { "type": "paragraph", "text": "The greetings above begin with mu- or mw-, which addresses several people, or one person you want to show respect to. With a single friend or a child, the same words start with u- or w- instead." },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "tableHeaders": ["Kinyarwanda", "English"],
        "tableRows": [
          ["Waramutse", "Good morning (to one friend)"],
          ["Wiriwe", "Good afternoon / evening (to one friend)"],
          ["Uraho", "Hello (to one friend)"],
          ["Urakoze", "Thank you (to one friend)"]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "dialogue",
        "dialogue": [
          { "speaker": "Keza", "swahili": "Mwaramutse, Eric!", "english": "Good morning, Eric!" },
          { "speaker": "Eric", "swahili": "Mwaramutse, Keza. Amakuru?", "english": "Good morning, Keza. How are you?" },
          { "speaker": "Keza", "swahili": "Ni meza, murakoze. Nawe?", "english": "Fine, thank you. And you?" },
          { "speaker": "Eric", "swahili": "Ni meza cyane.", "english": "Very well." }
        ]
      },
      { "type": "heading", "heading": "Saying Goodbye" },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "tableHeaders": ["Kinyarwanda", "English"],
        "tableRows": [
          ["Mwirirwe", "Have a good day"],
          ["Muramuke", "Good night"],
          ["Ni ah'ejo", "See you tomorrow"],
          ["Murabeho", "Goodbye (for a long time)"]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "callout",
        "calloutTitle": "Yes and no",
        "lines": ["Yego is 'yes' and oya is 'no'. Both are used on their own, exactly like the English words."]
      },
      {
        "type": "quickCheck",
        "calloutTitle": "Quick check",
        "questions": [
          { "prompt": "It is 8 a.m. and you meet your teacher. You say:", "options": ["Mwaramutse", "Mwiriwe", "Muramuke", "Murabeho"], "answer": "Mwaramutse", "explain": "Mwaramutse is the morning greeting." },
          { "prompt": "Someone asks 'Amakuru?'. You answer:", "options": ["Ni meza", "Oya", "Murabeho", "Bite"], "answer": "Ni meza", "explain": "Amakuru asks for your news; Ni meza says it is good." },
          { "prompt": "Which form thanks one close friend?", "options": ["Urakoze", "Murakoze", "Uraho", "Mwiriwe"], "answer": "Urakoze", "explain": "The u- forms address one person informally." }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "vocabulary": [
      { "swahili": "mwaramutse", "english": "good morning" },
      { "swahili": "mwiriwe", "english": "good afternoon / evening" },
      { "swahili": "muraho", "english": "hello" },
      { "swahili": "bite", "english": "hi / what's up" },
      { "swahili": "amakuru", "english": "news / how are you" },
      { "swahili": "ni meza", "english": "fine" },
      { "swahili": "murakoze", "english": "thank you" },
      { "swahili": "cyane", "english": "very / a lot" },
      { "swahili": "mwirirwe", "english": "have a good day" },
      { "swahili": "muramuke", "english": "good night" },
      { "swahili": "murabeho", "english": "goodbye" },
      { "swahili": "yego", "english": "yes" },
      { "swahili": "oya", "english": "no" }
    ],
    "quiz": [
      { "id": 6, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "How do you say 'Good morning' to a group?", "options": ["Mwiriwe", "Mwaramutse", "Muramuke", "Murakoze"], "answer": "Mwaramutse", "hint": null },
      { "id": 7, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "What is the usual reply to 'Amakuru?'", "options": ["Yego", "Murabeho", "Ni meza", "Bite"], "answer": "Ni meza", "hint": null },
      { "id": 8, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "'Murakoze cyane' means:", "options": ["Good night", "Thank you very much", "See you tomorrow", "How are you?"], "answer": "Thank you very much", "hint": null },
      { "id": 9, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "Which word means 'no'?", "options": ["Yego", "Oya", "Bite", "Cyane"], "answer": "Oya", "hint": null },
      { "id": 10, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "When is 'Muraho' the right greeting?", "options": ["Every morning", "When you have not seen someone for a while", "Only at night", "Only when leaving"], "answer": "When you have not seen someone for a while", "hint": null },
      { "id": 11, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "You are leaving in the evening and want to say 'Good night':", "options": ["Muramuke", "Mwaramutse", "Muraho", "Amakuru"], "answer": "Muramuke", "hint": null }
    ],
    "xpReward": 100
  },
  {
    "id": 3,
    "week": 1,
    "title": "Introducing Yourself",
    "subtitle": "Your name, where you are from, where you live",
    "theme": "Introductions",
    "blocks": [
      { "type": "heading", "heading": "Names" },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "tableHeaders": ["Kinyarwanda", "English"],
        "tableRows": [
          ["Witwa nde?", "What is your name?"],
          ["Nitwa Anna", "My name is Anna"],
          ["Nishimiye kukumenya", "Pleased to meet you"],
          ["Nanjye", "Me too"]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "callout",
        "calloutTitle": "The person is at the front of the verb",
        "lines": [
          "Kinyarwanda does not need separate words for 'I' and 'you'. The verb itself begins with n- for 'I' and u- (or w- before a vowel) for 'you'.",
          "Nitwa — I am called. Witwa — you are called. The rest of the word stays the same."
        ]
      },
      { "type": "heading", "heading": "Where You Are From, Where You Live" },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "tableHeaders": ["Kinyarwanda", "English"],
        "tableRows": [
          ["Ukomoka he?", "Where are you from?"],
          ["Nkomoka muri Kanada", "I am from Canada"],
          ["Utuye he?", "Where do you live?"],
          ["Ntuye i Kigali", "I live in Kigali"]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "callout",
        "calloutTitle": "muri for countries, i for towns",
        "lines": [
          "Use muri (or mu) before a country and i before a town or city: muri Kanada, mu Rwanda, i Kigali, i Londoni.",
          "He? means 'where?' and comes at the end of the question."
        ]
      },
      { "type": "heading", "heading": "I Am, You Are" },
      { "type": "paragraph", "text": "The same n- and u- appear in the verb 'to be': ndi is 'I am' and uri is 'you are'." },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "lines": [
          "Ndi umunyeshuri — I am a student",
          "Ndi umwarimu — I am a teacher",
          "Uri umunyeshuri? — Are you a student?"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "dialogue",
        "dialogue": [
          { "speaker": "Eric", "swahili": "Bite! Witwa nde?", "english": "Hi! What is your name?" },
          { "speaker": "Anna", "swahili": "Nitwa Anna. Nawe witwa nde?", "english": "My name is Anna. And what is yours?" },
          { "speaker": "Eric", "swahili": "Nitwa Eric. Ukomoka he?", "english": "My name is Eric. Where are you from?" },
          { "speaker": "Anna", "swahili": "Nkomoka muri Kanada, ariko ntuye i Kigali.", "english": "I am from Canada, but I live in Kigali." },
          { "speaker": "Eric", "swahili": "Nishimiye kukumenya, Anna.", "english": "Pleased to meet you, Anna." },
          { "speaker": "Anna", "swahili": "Nanjye. Murakoze!", "english": "Me too. Thank you!" }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "quickCheck",
        "calloutTitle": "Quick check",
        "questions": [
          { "prompt": "How do you say 'My name is Sam'?", "options": ["Nitwa Sam", "Witwa Sam", "Ntuye Sam", "Uri Sam"], "answer": "Nitwa Sam", "explain": "n- at the front of the verb is 'I'." },
          { "prompt": "Which word goes before a town name?", "options": ["i", "muri", "he", "ndi"], "answer": "i", "explain": "i Kigali, but muri Kanada." },
          { "prompt": "'Ukomoka he?' asks:", "options": ["Where are you from?", "What is your name?", "How are you?", "Where do you live?"], "answer": "Where are you from?", "explain": "Ukomoka — you come from; he? — where?" }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "vocabulary": [
      { "swahili": "nitwa", "english": "my name is" },
      { "swahili": "witwa nde?", "english": "what is your name?" },
      { "swahili": "nkomoka", "english": "I am from" },
      { "swahili": "ukomoka he?", "english": "where are you from?" },
      { "swahili": "ntuye", "english": "I live" },
      { "swahili": "utuye he?", "english": "where do you live?" },
      { "swahili": "ndi", "english": "I am" },
      { "swahili": "uri", "english": "you are" },
      { "swahili": "umunyeshuri", "english": "student" },
      { "swahili": "umwarimu", "english": "teacher" },
      { "swahili": "ariko", "english": "but" },
      { "swahili": "nanjye", "english": "me too" }
    ],
    "quiz": [
      { "id": 12, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "What does 'Witwa nde?' mean?", "options": ["Where do you live?", "What is your name?", "How are you?", "Are you a student?"], "answer": "What is your name?", "hint": null },
      { "id": 13, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "How do you say 'I live in Kigali'?", "options": ["Nkomoka i Kigali", "Utuye i Kigali", "Ntuye i Kigali", "Ndi i Kigali he"], "answer": "Ntuye i Kigali", "hint": null },
      { "id": 14, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "'Ndi umwarimu' means:", "options": ["You are a teacher", "I am a student", "I am a teacher", "My name is Mwarimu"], "answer": "I am a teacher", "hint": null },
      { "id": 15, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "Which letter at the front of a verb means 'you'?", "options": ["n-", "u-", "i-", "a-"], "answer": "u-", "hint": null },
      { "id": 16, "type": "multiple_choice", "prompt": "What does 'ariko' mean?", "options": ["And", "But", "Yes", "Where"], "answer": "But", "hint": null }
    ],
    "xpReward": 100
  }
]
